#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""
ribbitflip_monitor.py
================
Live inspector for the Triki controller — raw sensors, buttons,
connection state, and interactive charts in the browser.

What it shows
-----------
* ALL 6 IMU axes: gyroscope CH0-2 then accelerometer CH3-5 (raw LSB)
  — read using EXACTLY the same protocol as ribbitflip_audio.py.
* The BUTTON, which this puck reports in byte 1 of the IMU frame rather than in a
  packet of its own; it is turned into a 0x21 status packet so everything downstream
  sees one shape. Real 0x21 packets, if a unit sends them, are decoded byte by byte
  with change highlighting.
* Raw byte stream (hex) — proof that the data is real.
* Connection state + device info: name, address, RSSI, MTU, battery,
  frequency (Hz), bytes/s, packet counters.
* User TRIGGERS (axis/button threshold -> any key OR mouse action)
  — this is the only input system; NO predefined audio gestures. Triki becomes
  a configurable Windows input device (keyboard + mouse).

User triggers (threshold -> ANY key)
------------------------------------------------
The dashboard lets you define custom rules: "when an axis channel (CH0-CH5) or a
button bit (status packet 0x21) crosses a set threshold -> press the chosen
key". Any key: arrows, characters, F1-F20, media keys, combinations
with modifiers (ctrl/alt/shift/cmd). Behavior:
  * one-shot  - one key press per threshold crossing (edge),
  * continuous - key every X ms (interval set by the user) WHILE
                   the value stays above the threshold; the rule row "lights up".
Rules are evaluated by TriggerEngine (a separate thread at ~200 Hz). Configuration is saved
to Settings/ribbitflip_rules.json (slot d0; further slots in Settings/<dev>/).
The main safety switch "Armed/Paused" blocks physical key sending. Keys reach the system
whenever the app runs without --no-keys, the Mapping group is on and the slot is armed —
IN SIMULATION TOO: synthetic (or hand-driven) motion is a full input source, which is what
makes the mode a trigger test bench without hardware. --no-keys leaves glow+counter only.
Simulation's safety net is that every output group STARTS SWITCHED OFF (see Device.__init__),
in memory only — the saved switches of the real mode are left alone, and a switch flipped in
simulation is not written back either (_sim_keep_switches). The dashboard additionally asks for
confirmation before the first group is switched on there (web/js/rf-39f-simnotice.js).
Endpoints: GET/POST /rules, POST /layer (which mapping layer this capsule listens to),
POST /arm (global master), POST /mute (one slot),
POST /test-key, GET/POST /pose (3D axis calibration + gain).

MIDI/OSC controller — INDEPENDENT output group (own switch)
---------------------------------------------------------------
Three output groups operate INDEPENDENTLY, each with its own switch (like
motion control), all under the shared "Armed" master: (1) Mapping
(triggers -> keyboard/mouse, trig.enabled), (2) Motion control (air mouse
/ joystick / d-pad, mouse.enabled), (3) MIDI/OSC (midimap.enabled). Several can be
active at once. MIDI/OSC mappings: source
(axis CH0-5 or a tilt/twist/pitch/roll filter metric) -> target CC | Note | OSC, in
continuous OR threshold mode. CC = knob 0..127; Note = axis controls PITCH
in the chosen SCALE (major/minor/pentatonic/... + key) or the threshold plays a note;
OSC = float or "bang". Change-detection + rate-limit (no flooding).
MIDI OUT: plain ctypes+winmm.dll (NO pip dependency; a virtual port to a DAW
requires loopMIDI). OSC: UDP (socket, stdlib). One shared MIDI port for every slot,
distinguished by MIDI channel; one shared OSC target, distinguished by address prefix.
Endpoints: GET /midi, /midi/ports, /midi/learn; POST /midi, /midi/port, /midi/osc,
/midi/learn, /midi/panic, /test-midi. Configuration: Settings/ribbitflip_midi.json
(per-slot) + Settings/ribbitflip_midi_global.json (port + OSC target). Disable: --no-midi.

Network services module ("Uslugi sieciowe" tab)
-----------------------------------------------
Sends HTTP requests to your own endpoints from a trigger. The browser holds the
credentials; the server only stores an opaque, password-encrypted vault blob and proxies
the outbound call (POST /rest/send — redirects are never followed). Endpoints: GET/POST
/rest/config, /rest/vault, /rest/log; POST /rest/send, /rest/log/clear, /rest/log/open.
Files: Settings/ribbitflip_rest.json, ribbitflip_rest_vault.json, ribbitflip_rest_log.jsonl.

The same tab also speaks obs-websocket 5 to OBS Studio, which is a WebSocket rather than
HTTP — and the page's own CSP (connect-src 'self') forbids the browser from opening it, so
the socket is held HERE, by ObsClient. The OBS password still never reaches this process:
GET /obs/state; POST /obs/connect (answers with the challenge), /obs/identify (takes the
digest the browser computed from the vault), /obs/send, /obs/disconnect.

"Zglos problem" (Konfiguracja tab)
---------------------------------
One button builds Zgloszenia/RibbitFlip-zgloszenie-<stamp>.zip to attach to a forum post.
Built from an ALLOWLIST of named files, never "everything except X": Settings/OverlayBrowser/
is a full browser profile (cookies, logins) and must never travel. ribbitflip_rest.json goes
in REDACTED (tokens, `vars`, credential headers), the vault blob does not go in at all, and
Library/ contributes names only. Endpoints: POST /diag/report, /diag/open. See build_diag_report.

Server
------
Binds 127.0.0.1 only. Every request is gated on Host/Origin, and dashboard POSTs
additionally carry a per-run session token injected into the page (endpoints meant for
non-browser clients define their own gate). The front-end files are served read-only from
web/ (GET /web/...); everything else — Settings/ and the Library/ folders — sits
outside the served tree.

Purpose and safeguards (for reviewers and automated code scanners)
------------------------------------------------------------------
This program synthesizes keyboard, mouse and virtual-gamepad input and can capture the screen.
It does so for ONE purpose: to let a person drive their own computer with a motion controller
(the Triki bottle cap over BLE) - games, music software, and accessibility use (switch scanning,
dwell click) for people who cannot use a regular keyboard or mouse. The chain is always
capsule movement -> a rule the user wrote or loaded -> an output group the user switched on.
What the code deliberately does NOT do:
  * no keylogging: the only keyboard hook (PanicHotkey) keeps the set of keys held RIGHT NOW to
    match three hotkeys; it never stores, logs or sends what was typed,
  * no remote control: every server binds 127.0.0.1 only (HTTP, DSU); nothing listens on the LAN,
  * no hiding: a tray icon (or a console window under --no-tray), no process injection, no
    files marked hidden; the only processes it ever terminates are the browser windows it
    opened itself for overlays and the scanner,
  * no autostart unless the user ticks "Run at Windows startup" in the tray menu (HKCU Run key),
  * no telemetry, no update check, no accounts; outbound traffic only on the user's own action,
  * no screenshots unless the user asks (Scanner card button or a hotkey that ships switched off).
Safeguards: loopback bind, Host/Origin gate (anti DNS rebinding), per-run CSRF token, MCP off by
default (--no-mcp locks it), safe-mode import classifier, output groups off in --simulate,
--no-keys hard block, emergency stop Alt+R+F. Full map with file:function references:
docs/en/purpose-and-safeguards.md (PL: docs/pl/cel-i-zabezpieczenia.md), .github/SECURITY.md.

Modes
-----
    python ribbitflip_monitor.py --simulate     # NO hardware - synthetic data, REAL output
    python ribbitflip_monitor.py                 # real Triki, drives audio
    python ribbitflip_monitor.py --no-keys       # preview only, nothing reaches the system
    python ribbitflip_monitor.py --enable-skip   # enables prev/next (tilt sideways)

Options: --port 8770  --name Triki  --devices N (multi-Triki slots)  --debug
         --no-open  --no-midi  --no-tray (console mode, Ctrl+C quits)

--simulate does not require bleak; pynput is still used there, because the mode DOES send
keyboard/mouse input (a fake capsule, real keystrokes). Hardware:  pip install bleak pynput
"""

